Q: why don&#39;t C overflow checks use CPU flags? A: <p>You can use C23 <em>Checked Integer Arithmetic</em> (see C23 7.20)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If these type-generic macros return <code>false</code>, the value assigned to <code>*result</code> correctly represents the mathematical result of the operation. Otherwise, these type-generic macros return <code>true</code>. In this case, the value assigned to <code>*result</code> is the mathematical result of the operation wrapped around to the width of <code>*result</code>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;limits.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdckdint.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
int main(void) {
    int res;
    if (ckd_add(&amp;res, INT_MAX, INT_MAX)) printf(&quot;wrap around ==&gt; &quot;);
    printf(&quot;%d\n&quot;, res);

    long long llres;
    if (ckd_add(&amp;llres, INT_MIN, INT_MIN)) printf(&quot;wrap around ==&gt; &quot;);
    printf(&quot;%lld\n&quot;, llres);
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/zWd9c31Wf" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://godbolt.org/z/zWd9c31Wf</a></p>

Q: Global `comptime var` in Zig A: <p><sub><em>Note: code in this answer tested with 0.15.2. Content should be relevant to most older versions</em></sub></p>
<p>tl;dr:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>var num: usize = 0;
</code></pre>
<p>or, preferably:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>const Globals = @This();
num: usize = 0,

fn foo(self: *Globals) void {
    self.num += 1;
}

fn bar(self: *Globals) void {
    self.num += 2;
}
</code></pre>
<h1>How to make the &quot;Global Variable&quot; work</h1>
<p>Here are some things that might help you understand why this doesn't work.</p>
<h2>Implicit file structs</h2>
<p>First, <a href="https://ziglang.org/documentation/0.15.2/#toc-Source-File-Structs" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><strong>all Zig source files are implicit <code>struct</code> declarations</strong></a>. This means that <code>num</code> here is technically a container level declaration. This is important as there is no such thing as a global variable in Zig. Every declaration has an associated container. This means your example source file is roughly equivalent to this struct.</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>struct {
    // This doesn't compile
    comptime var num: comptime_int = 0;

    fn foo() void {
        num += 1;
    }

    fn bar() void {
        num += 2;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Since all container-level declarations are implicitly comptime expressions, the <code>comptime</code> keyword is not allowed on them, as it would be redundant. Therefore, we should first remove the keyword entirely (although this will not compile yet):</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>// This doesn't compile yet
var num: comptime_int = 0;
</code></pre>
<h2><code>comptime_int</code> must be compile-time known</h2>
<p>Next, <a href="https://ziglang.org/documentation/0.15.2/#Primitive-Types" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><strong>the <code>comptime_int</code> type is only allowed for comptime-known values.</strong></a>. This is because the <code>comptime_int</code> type has no fixed size. This is why this compile error occurs after we update our code:</p>
<pre><code>code.zig:1:10: error: variable of type 'comptime_int' must be const or comptime
var num: comptime_int = 0;
         ^~~~~~~~~~~~
code.zig:1:10: note: to modify this variable at runtime, it must be given an explicit fixed-size number type
</code></pre>
<p>Whenever a number is incremented, there is a chance it overflows its allocated space. At compile time, <code>comptime_int</code> types are only as big as they need to be. At runtime, types cannot be arbitrarily resized. Therefore, the size must become known so the program can detect integer overflows.</p>
<p>To remedy this problem, we simply need to retype <code>num</code> to a fixed sized integer:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>// usize is always the size of a pointer for the current compile target
var num: usize = 0;
</code></pre>
<p>We can now test our code and see that it works:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>var num: usize = 0;

fn foo() void {
    num += 1;
}

fn bar() void {
    num += 2;
}

test {
    const testing = @import(&quot;std&quot;).testing;
    foo();
    bar();

    try testing.expectEqual(3, num);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre><code>$ zig test code.zig
All 1 tests passed.
</code></pre>
<h1>The correct way to do this</h1>
<p>The &quot;global variable&quot; method is likely not the behavior you intended. Even though the <code>num</code> declaration is a compile-time expression, its value is now completely runtime. If we try to call <code>foo()</code> or <code>bar()</code> at compile time, we get a compile error. Updating our test block exemplifies this:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>test {
    const testing = @import(&quot;std&quot;).testing;
    comptime foo();

    try comptime testing.expectEqual(1, num);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre><code>$ zig test code.zig
code.zig:4:9: error: unable to evaluate comptime expression
    num += 1;
    ~~~~^~~~
code.zig:4:5: note: operation is runtime due to this operand
    num += 1;
    ^~~
code.zig:13:17: note: called at comptime from here
    comptime foo();
             ~~~^~
code.zig:13:5: note: 'comptime' keyword forces comptime evaluation
    comptime foo();
    ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~
</code></pre>
<p>If we really want compile time and runtime compatibility, we need to change <code>num</code> from a <code>var</code> declaration to a struct field on the implicit file struct:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>// This is how we can name implicit file structs
// If preferred, you can also use @This() directly
// The name &quot;Globals&quot; isn't special here
const Globals = @This();

// This is now a field on the implicit struct
num: usize = 0,

// These functions must now accept pointers to self
// These must be pointers since arguments are immutable
fn foo(self: *Globals) void {
    self.num += 1;
}

fn bar(self: *Globals) void {
    self.num += 2;
}

const testing = @import(&quot;std&quot;).testing;

test &quot;Comptime Only&quot; {
    comptime {
        var globals: Globals = .{};
        globals.foo();
        globals.bar();
        try testing.expectEqual(3, globals.num);
    }
}

test &quot;Runtime Only&quot; {
    var globals: Globals = .{};
    globals.foo();
    globals.bar();
    try testing.expectEqual(3, globals.num);
}

test &quot;Mixed comptime and runtime&quot; {
    var globals: Globals = comptime blk: {
        var globals: Globals = .{};
        globals.foo();
        globals.bar();
        try testing.expectEqual(3, globals.num);
        break :blk globals;
    };
    try testing.expectEqual(3, globals.num);
    globals.foo();
    globals.bar();
    try testing.expectEqual(6, globals.num);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre><code>$ zig test code.zig
All 3 tests passed.
</code></pre>
<h2>Why this method is preferred</h2>
<p>Zig is a language designed for reading over writing. This means the source code should leave no ambiguity for the reader as to what it does. Global variables in most languages secretly create a namespace which is not exposed to the reader. This leaves ambiguity in languages that allow compile-time execution, since the reader needs to determine whether a reference to <code>num</code> refers to its compile-time value or its runtime value.</p>
<p>Zig avoids this ambiguity by disabling &quot;global variable access&quot;, or container-level variable declaration access, at compile time, with the only exception of setting an initial value. If you want a value to be usable at both compile-time and runtime, you must explicitly define the &quot;global state&quot; as a struct. You can then make a single &quot;global&quot; instance of this struct and initialize it at compile-time using the same functions that modify the state at runtime.</p>
